window.QUIZ_BANK['selenium'] = (window.QUIZ_BANK['selenium']||[]).concat([
{id:4281,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'In a hybrid testing approach, what does "API+UI" testing mean?',options:['A) Running API tests inside a browser','B) Using API calls to set up state and UI automation to verify the result','C) Testing only REST endpoints with Selenium','D) Replacing all UI tests with API tests'],answer:1,explanation:'Hybrid API+UI testing uses fast API calls (e.g., REST) to create preconditions or verify back-end state while Selenium handles the visual/interactive layers, reducing test brittleness and execution time.'},
{id:4282,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which layer in a three-layer Selenium architecture is responsible for raw WebDriver commands?',options:['A) Business layer','B) Test layer','C) Technical/infrastructure layer','D) Reporting layer'],answer:2,explanation:'The three-layer architecture separates concerns: the technical layer wraps raw WebDriver interactions, the business layer expresses user workflows, and the test layer contains assertions. This keeps WebDriver API changes isolated to one place.'},
{id:4283,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How can a Selenium test detect whether a feature flag is enabled before deciding which UI path to exercise?',options:['A) By inspecting the page title only','B) By calling the feature-flag API/config endpoint and branching test logic accordingly','C) By hardcoding all feature states in the test','D) Feature flags cannot be used with Selenium'],answer:1,explanation:'Tests can query a feature-flag service (LaunchDarkly, Unleash, etc.) via HTTP before the UI interaction, then conditionally follow the enabled or disabled code path, keeping a single test valid for both flag states.'},
{id:4284,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the primary goal of a "smoke" test category in a Selenium suite?',options:['A) Exhaustive regression of all flows','B) Quick sanity check that critical paths are working after a deployment','C) Performance benchmarking','D) Security scanning'],answer:1,explanation:'Smoke tests are a small, fast subset that verify the most critical application paths are functional. They are typically the first gate in a CI pipeline to catch show-stopper defects before deeper testing runs.'},
{id:4285,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which Selenium 4 class is used to enable browser performance logging for measuring network events?',options:['A) LoggingPreferences','B) PerformanceLoggingPreferences','C) NetworkInterceptor','D) DevToolsService'],answer:1,explanation:'`LoggingPreferences` (set via `ChromeOptions.setCapability`) with `LogType.PERFORMANCE` enabled collects browser performance entries. In older Selenium 3 ChromeDriver the dedicated class was `PerformanceLoggingPreferences`; Selenium 4 unifies this under `LoggingPreferences`.'},
{id:4286,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'When deploying Selenium Grid 4 on Kubernetes with Helm, which component handles dynamic node scaling?',options:['A) The Hub','B) The Router','C) KEDA (Kubernetes Event-Driven Autoscaler) combined with the Grid autoscaler','D) The Distributor only'],answer:2,explanation:'The official Selenium Grid 4 Helm chart integrates with KEDA, which watches the Grid queue depth and scales Node Deployments up or down automatically, allowing cost-efficient elastic capacity on Kubernetes clusters.'},
{id:4287,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the Monte Screen Recorder library allow you to do within a Selenium test?',options:['A) Take single screenshots','B) Record full video of the browser session for debugging','C) Stream the browser to a remote viewer','D) Capture only network requests'],answer:1,explanation:'Monte Screen Recorder is a Java library that captures the screen (or a region) as an AVI/MP4 during test execution. Starting the recorder before a test and stopping it after produces a video artifact useful for diagnosing intermittent failures.'},
{id:4288,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which CSS property change should a Selenium test verify when checking right-to-left (RTL) language rendering?',options:['A) color','B) direction:rtl and/or text-align:right','C) font-size','D) z-index'],answer:1,explanation:'RTL languages (Arabic, Hebrew) require `direction: rtl` on the document or container element. Selenium can assert `element.getCssValue("direction").equals("rtl")` and verify that text flows correctly from right to left.'},
{id:4289,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you switch the application language in a Selenium localization test?',options:['A) Change the OS language','B) Interact with the language-switcher UI element or set a locale cookie/query parameter','C) Reinstall the browser','D) Modify the JVM locale only'],answer:1,explanation:'Most web apps expose a language switcher (dropdown or link) or accept a `lang` query parameter or cookie. Selenium clicks the switcher and then asserts that translated strings appear, or injects a locale cookie before navigation.'},
{id:4290,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In a Single Page Application, what is a "soft navigation" and why does it matter for Selenium waits?',options:['A) A full page reload triggered by the back button','B) A client-side route change that updates the URL and DOM without a browser page load event','C) A navigation inside an iframe','D) Any navigation over HTTP/2'],answer:1,explanation:'Soft navigations use the History API (pushState) and do not fire a browser `load` event. Selenium\'s `ExpectedConditions.urlContains` or explicit waits on new DOM elements must be used instead of relying on page-load completion signals.'},
{id:4291,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the recommended workaround for HTML5 drag-and-drop when the built-in `Actions.dragAndDrop()` fails on modern browsers?',options:['A) Use the Select class','B) Inject a JavaScript snippet that dispatches custom dragstart/dragover/drop events','C) Use a keyboard shortcut instead','D) Disable HTML5 drag-and-drop in browser flags'],answer:1,explanation:'Many HTML5 drag implementations use synthetic events not fully replicated by WebDriver\'s Actions API. The common fix is executing a JS helper (e.g., the "drag-and-drop-helper.js" snippet) that programmatically fires `dragstart`, `dragover`, and `drop` events on the elements.'},
{id:4292,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How can Selenium verify that the Tab key correctly moves focus through interactive elements in the expected order?',options:['A) By reading the DOM order and comparing to design docs','B) By sending Keys.TAB repeatedly and asserting `driver.switchTo().activeElement()` at each step','C) By inspecting tabindex attributes only','D) Tab order cannot be tested with Selenium'],answer:1,explanation:'`Actions.sendKeys(Keys.TAB)` moves focus to the next focusable element. After each TAB, `driver.switchTo().activeElement()` returns the currently focused element, allowing assertion of its tag, role, or data attribute for correct keyboard navigation order.'},
{id:4293,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What JavaScript approach allows a Selenium test to read from or write to the system clipboard?',options:['A) driver.manage().clipboard()','B) Executing `navigator.clipboard.writeText()/readText()` via JavascriptExecutor','C) Using the Clipboard class from java.awt','D) Clipboard cannot be accessed in automated browsers'],answer:1,explanation:'In headless/automated browsers, the Clipboard API requires permission. Tests can call `navigator.clipboard.writeText(text)` via `JavascriptExecutor` or grant clipboard permissions via CDP, then read back with `navigator.clipboard.readText()` to assert clipboard content.'},
{id:4294,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the typical strategy to assert that a PDF file has been downloaded correctly in a Selenium test?',options:['A) Visually verify it in the browser PDF viewer','B) Configure the browser to auto-download to a known directory, then verify the file exists and check its size or content','C) Use a Selenium PDF API','D) Download testing is impossible with Selenium'],answer:1,explanation:'Chrome/Firefox profiles can be configured to save PDFs to a specific download folder without the dialog. After clicking the download link, Selenium polls the directory for the expected file and optionally uses a PDF library (Apache PDFBox) to assert content.'},
{id:4295,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you measure Time To First Byte (TTFB) using Selenium 4 and the Chrome DevTools Protocol?',options:['A) By timing the `driver.get()` call with System.currentTimeMillis()','B) By using DevTools `Network.responseReceived` event or reading the Navigation Timing API via JavaScript','C) TTFB is only measurable with JMeter','D) Using PerformanceTiming.loadEventEnd'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium 4\'s CDP integration allows subscribing to `Network.responseReceived` events which carry `timing.receiveHeadersEnd` (TTFB). Alternatively, `window.performance.timing.responseStart - window.performance.timing.requestStart` gives TTFB via JavaScript execution.'},
{id:4296,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What CI/CD "result badge" shows stakeholders the current status of a Selenium test suite?',options:['A) A JUnit XML attachment','B) A dynamic SVG/PNG badge (e.g., from Shields.io or the CI system) that reflects the latest build pass/fail status','C) A screenshot in the PR description','D) A Slack message'],answer:1,explanation:'Build badges embed the latest pipeline outcome as an image URL in README files or dashboards. Services like GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Jenkins, and Shields.io generate these badges automatically from build results.'},
{id:4297,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In a data-driven Selenium test, how can test inputs be sourced directly from a database?',options:['A) Only CSV files can be used','B) By executing JDBC queries in a `@DataProvider` (TestNG) or `@MethodSource` (JUnit 5) method to supply rows as test parameters','C) The database must be exported to Excel first','D) Databases are not usable with Selenium'],answer:1,explanation:'A `@DataProvider` method opens a JDBC connection, executes a SELECT, maps each row to an `Object[]`, and returns a 2D array. Selenium then runs the test for each row, enabling production-like data sets without manual maintenance of test files.'},
{id:4298,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which AssertJ method provides a fluent way to check that a WebElement\'s text contains a substring?',options:['A) assertThat(element).hasText()','B) assertThat(element.getText()).contains("expected")','C) assertEqual(element.getText(), "expected")','D) element.assertContains("expected")'],answer:1,explanation:'AssertJ\'s `assertThat(String).contains(CharSequence)` reads naturally as fluent English and produces descriptive failure messages. Combining it with `element.getText()` allows rich assertions like `assertThat(el.getText()).containsIgnoringCase("Welcome")`.'},
{id:4299,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does Serenity BDD differ from plain Selenium in terms of reporting?',options:['A) Serenity only generates JUnit XML','B) Serenity auto-generates living documentation reports with step-by-step screenshots and business-readable narrative','C) Serenity uses a different browser driver','D) Serenity does not support Selenium'],answer:1,explanation:'Serenity wraps Selenium calls in annotated `@Step` methods and generates rich HTML reports with aggregated results, Cucumber narrative, screenshots per step, and coverage statistics — far beyond what standard Selenium reporters produce.'},
{id:4300,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the main advantage of FluentSelenium over plain WebDriver API?',options:['A) It replaces WebDriver completely','B) It provides a fluent/chained API that reduces boilerplate and improves readability of element interactions','C) It adds AI-based locator healing','D) It only works with Firefox'],answer:1,explanation:'FluentSelenium wraps WebDriver in a chainable DSL: `fwd.element(By.id("x")).click().element(By.id("y")).sendKeys("z")`. It also adds implicit retry and assertion helpers, reducing the verbosity of typical Selenium test code.'},
{id:4301,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the WebDriver BiDi specification and how does it differ from the classic WebDriver (W3C) protocol?',options:['A) BiDi is identical to W3C WebDriver but uses GraphQL','B) BiDi adds a bidirectional event-driven channel (WebSocket) alongside the existing HTTP command channel, enabling real-time browser events','C) BiDi replaces HTTP commands entirely with gRPC','D) BiDi is a Selenium Grid protocol only'],answer:1,explanation:'Classic WebDriver is strictly request/response over HTTP. BiDi (WebDriver BiDirectional Protocol) adds a persistent WebSocket channel allowing the browser to push events (console logs, network events, DOM mutations) to the client asynchronously without polling.'},
{id:4302,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How does Applitools Eyes assist with "AI-assisted locator" healing in Selenium tests?',options:['A) It rewrites locators automatically when the DOM changes using visual AI matching','B) It only takes screenshots and emails them','C) It replaces Selenium with its own driver','D) It generates XPath from production traffic'],answer:0,explanation:'Applitools Eyes uses visual AI to match elements by appearance rather than fragile CSS/XPath selectors. Its SDK can identify elements even when their DOM attributes change, reducing locator maintenance and flaky failures caused by minor UI refactors.'},
{id:4303,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'When automating an OAuth2 Authorization Code flow with Selenium, what is the most reliable approach to avoid CAPTCHA/bot detection on the IdP login page?',options:['A) Solve CAPTCHA with OCR','B) Exchange credentials directly via the OAuth2 token endpoint (Resource Owner Password Credentials grant) and inject the resulting token cookie','C) Disable JavaScript on the IdP','D) Use proxy intercept to skip the IdP entirely'],answer:1,explanation:'Automating a real IdP UI risks bot detection and CAPTCHA. The ROPC grant or client credentials flow returns an access token via HTTP without a browser. Tests inject this token as a cookie or Authorization header, bypassing the IdP UI while still testing the protected application.'},
{id:4304,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the safest way to inject a JWT into a browser session in a Selenium test?',options:['A) Append it to the URL as a query parameter','B) Use `document.cookie` via JavascriptExecutor or `driver.manage().addCookie()` to set the auth cookie after navigating to the domain','C) Embed it in the page title','D) Store it in localStorage via WebDriver\'s built-in API'],answer:1,explanation:'`driver.manage().addCookie(new Cookie("token", jwtValue))` sets a cookie on the current domain without a UI login flow. For localStorage tokens, `((JavascriptExecutor)driver).executeScript("localStorage.setItem(\'token\', arguments[0]);", jwt)` achieves the same.'},
{id:4305,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which KPI best measures the reliability of a Selenium test suite over time?',options:['A) Number of test files','B) Test pass rate (%) across the last N builds, tracking flaky test frequency','C) Total lines of test code','D) Browser version number'],answer:1,explanation:'Pass rate trending reveals suite health. Tracking it per build alongside flaky test rates (tests that flip pass/fail without code changes) provides actionable KPIs that drive maintenance prioritization and identify tests needing stabilization.'},
{id:4306,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the recommended "test data cleanup" pattern to prevent state leakage between Selenium tests?',options:['A) Restart the database before each test run','B) Use API calls or direct DB cleanup in @AfterEach/@AfterMethod to delete only data created by the test','C) Rely on the application to clean itself','D) Run all tests in a single browser session'],answer:1,explanation:'Targeted cleanup (delete the user/order/session created by the test) in `@AfterEach` keeps tests independent without requiring a full database reset. API-based cleanup is faster and less fragile than UI-driven cleanup.'},
{id:4307,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What does the `SameSite=None; Secure` cookie attribute requirement mean for Selenium tests involving cross-origin iframes?',options:['A) Cross-origin iframes are blocked entirely','B) Third-party cookies must be set with SameSite=None and Secure, otherwise the embedded iframe session is lost and Selenium may not authenticate','C) Selenium cannot access cross-origin iframes at all','D) The attribute only matters for mobile browsers'],answer:1,explanation:'Modern browsers enforce SameSite cookie policies. If an embedded iframe\'s auth cookie is missing SameSite=None; Secure, the iframe session is unauthenticated. Tests must ensure the cookie is set correctly (or use same-domain test environments) to avoid unexpected login redirects inside iframes.'},
{id:4308,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the key challenge when testing a browser extension with Selenium and how is it addressed?',options:['A) Extensions cannot be loaded in automated browsers','B) Chrome extensions must be loaded via ChromeOptions.addExtensions() with the .crx file; Firefox via FirefoxProfile.addExtension()','C) Extensions are only testable with Playwright','D) Extensions require physical user interaction'],answer:1,explanation:'ChromeOptions.addExtensions(file) loads a packed .crx into the Chrome instance started by WebDriver. Tests can then interact with extension-injected UI elements, background pages (via CDP targets), or side panels, enabling full extension automation.'},
{id:4309,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'Which Selenium 4 / CDP capability allows throttling network speed to simulate slow connections?',options:['A) driver.manage().timeouts().setNetworkLatency()','B) ChromeDriver\'s `Network.emulateNetworkConditions` CDP command','C) FirefoxDriver.setNetworkSpeed()','D) RemoteWebDriver.setThrottle()'],answer:1,explanation:'Via Selenium 4\'s `DevTools` API, calling `devTools.send(Network.emulateNetworkConditions(offline, latency, downloadThroughput, uploadThroughput, connectionType))` simulates network conditions (3G, offline, etc.) inside the Chrome session.'},
{id:4310,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is Gauge and how does it integrate with Selenium for test execution?',options:['A) Gauge is a Maven plugin for Selenium','B) Gauge is a BDD-style test framework where specifications are written in Markdown and step implementations use Selenium Java/Python','C) Gauge is a performance testing tool','D) Gauge generates Selenium locators automatically'],answer:1,explanation:'ThoughtWorks Gauge uses plain-text Markdown specs with step tags. Each step is implemented in Java (or other languages) using Selenium. Running `gauge run` executes specs and generates HTML/XML reports, offering a simpler alternative to Cucumber for BDD.'},
{id:4311,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which open-source tool is commonly used to create a Selenium test reporting dashboard aggregating results from multiple runs?',options:['A) Allure Report','B) Notepad','C) Swagger UI','D) Postman'],answer:0,explanation:'Allure Framework collects JUnit XML / Allure annotations from Selenium tests, stores history, and renders an interactive dashboard with trend charts, step-level screenshots, categories, and retry analytics — widely used as a Selenium reporting solution.'},
{id:4312,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'In a three-layer Selenium architecture, what does the "business layer" contain?',options:['A) Raw driver.findElement() calls','B) Reusable user-workflow methods such as `login(user, pass)` that call technical-layer methods','C) TestNG annotations','D) Database connection strings'],answer:1,explanation:'The business layer models application workflows in domain language (login, addToCart, checkout). It calls technical-layer helpers for element interactions, keeping test code readable and insulating business logic from WebDriver API changes.'},
{id:4313,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a common technique to test an application\'s behavior when a feature flag is off without modifying production configuration?',options:['A) Delete the feature flag from the database','B) Override the flag via a test-only API endpoint or environment variable before launching the browser session','C) Hardcode the flag state in the application source','D) Use a different browser'],answer:1,explanation:'Many feature-flag systems expose override mechanisms (cookies, request headers, or local storage keys) that force a specific flag state for a session. Tests set these overrides programmatically to exercise both the enabled and disabled code paths.'},
{id:4314,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does the "regression" test category differ from "smoke" tests in terms of scope and execution frequency?',options:['A) Regression tests are fewer and run after every commit','B) Regression tests are comprehensive, covering all features, and typically run on a scheduled basis or before major releases','C) Regression tests only test the login page','D) There is no difference'],answer:1,explanation:'Smoke tests are a small fast set; regression suites are broad, verifying that existing functionality is not broken by changes. Regressions often run nightly or on release branches due to their longer execution time.'},
{id:4315,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'Which Helm values control the number of Selenium Grid Chrome nodes in a Kubernetes deployment?',options:['A) grid.maxSession','B) chromeNode.replicas and chromeNode.maxSessions per pod','C) hub.nodeCount','D) selenium.parallelism'],answer:1,explanation:'In the official Selenium Grid Helm chart, `chromeNode.replicas` sets the number of Chrome Node pods and `chromeNode.maxSessions` (or `maxSessions` inside the node spec) controls concurrent sessions per pod. Multiplying both gives total Chrome capacity.'},
{id:4316,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'When using Monte Screen Recorder in a TestNG test, where is the recorder typically started and stopped?',options:['A) In the @DataProvider','B) In @BeforeMethod (start) and @AfterMethod (stop/save), so each test gets its own video file','C) In @BeforeSuite only','D) Monte cannot be used with TestNG'],answer:1,explanation:'Starting the GraphicsCapture recorder in `@BeforeMethod` and stopping/saving it in `@AfterMethod` produces per-test video files named after the test method, making it easy to link a specific video to a failing test in reports.'},
{id:4317,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What `lang` attribute assertion confirms that a page has loaded in Arabic (RTL) mode?',options:['A) document.charset == "UTF-8"','B) driver.findElement(By.tagName("html")).getAttribute("lang").equals("ar")','C) document.direction == "rtl"','D) Checking the window title'],answer:1,explanation:'The `<html lang="ar">` attribute is the standard indicator of Arabic locale. Asserting this attribute via WebDriver confirms the app responded to language switching correctly, and it can be combined with checking `dir="rtl"` on the body.'},
{id:4318,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the purpose of a `@DataProvider` in TestNG when combined with Selenium?',options:['A) To supply browser driver instances','B) To supply multiple sets of test inputs so the same test method runs with different data','C) To generate locators','D) To manage cookies'],answer:1,explanation:'`@DataProvider` returns a 2D array of parameters. TestNG invokes the `@Test` method once per row, enabling data-driven testing without duplicating test code — useful for testing the same UI flow with many input combinations.'},
{id:4319,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does Selenide simplify handling of dynamic elements compared to plain Selenium?',options:['A) Selenide removes the need for a browser','B) Selenide wraps WebDriver with automatic smart waits — each element interaction retries until the element is interactable or a timeout is reached','C) Selenide only works with static HTML','D) Selenide uses keyboard events exclusively'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenide\'s `$()` returns a lazy `SelenideElement` that retries all interactions (click, getText, shouldBe) with built-in waits. This eliminates most explicit `WebDriverWait` boilerplate while making tests more stable under slow rendering.'},
{id:4320,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'Which WebDriver BiDi event would you subscribe to in order to capture console errors in real-time during a Selenium 4 test?',options:['A) Log.entryAdded','B) Console.messageAdded','C) Runtime.consoleAPICalled','D) Both A and C depending on the browser'],answer:3,explanation:'In the BiDi spec, `Log.entryAdded` is the standardized event. Chrome DevTools Protocol uses `Runtime.consoleAPICalled`. Selenium 4\'s `DevTools.addListener(Log.entryAdded(...))` enables real-time console log capture, useful for detecting JS errors during test execution.'},
{id:4321,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the recommended way to handle an OAuth2 "implicit flow" callback URL redirect in a Selenium test?',options:['A) Manually copy the token from the URL bar','B) Capture driver.getCurrentUrl() after the redirect and parse the access_token fragment','C) OAuth2 implicit flow cannot be automated','D) Use a browser extension to intercept tokens'],answer:1,explanation:'After the IdP redirects to the callback URL with `#access_token=...`, `driver.getCurrentUrl()` returns the full URL including the fragment. Parsing it with `URI` or string manipulation extracts the token for subsequent authenticated API calls or cookie injection.'},
{id:4322,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Why is test execution time a critical KPI for Selenium suites in CI/CD?',options:['A) Longer tests are always more thorough','B) Slow test suites block PR merging and developer feedback loops, reducing deployment frequency','C) Execution time does not matter if tests pass','D) It only matters for mobile tests'],answer:1,explanation:'Fast feedback is core to CI/CD. Selenium suites exceeding 15–20 minutes stall developer workflows. Tracking execution time per suite/job over sprints helps identify regressions in test speed and justifies parallelization or pruning strategies.'},
{id:4323,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the correct way to simulate network offline mode using Selenium 4 CDP for Chrome?',options:['A) driver.manage().setOffline(true)','B) devTools.send(Network.emulateNetworkConditions(true, 0, -1, -1, Optional.of(ConnectionType.NONE)))','C) ChromeOptions.addArguments("--offline")','D) driver.executeScript("window.navigator.onLine = false")'],answer:1,explanation:'`Network.emulateNetworkConditions` with `offline=true` instructs Chrome via CDP to drop all network requests, simulating a complete loss of connectivity. The download/upload throughput values of -1 mean unlimited (ignored when offline=true).'},
{id:4324,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What file format does Gauge use to write test specifications?',options:['A) XML','B) JSON','C) Markdown (.md or .spec)','D) YAML'],answer:2,explanation:'Gauge specifications are written in plain Markdown-like .spec files. Steps are prefixed with `*` and correspond to annotated implementation methods. This makes specs human-readable without requiring knowledge of Gherkin or XML.'},
{id:4325,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which AssertJ assertion is best suited to verify that a list of web element texts matches an expected list in order?',options:['A) assertThat(texts).containsExactly("a","b","c")','B) assertEquals(texts, expected)','C) assertThat(texts).hasSize(3)','D) texts.forEach(Assert::assertNotNull)'],answer:0,explanation:'`containsExactly` verifies both element values AND order, failing with a diff message that shows what was expected vs. actual. It is the most precise assertion for ordered lists and produces more readable errors than JUnit\'s `assertEquals`.'},
{id:4326,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In a Selenium hybrid test, when should API calls be preferred over UI navigation for setting up state?',options:['A) Never — UI must be used for all actions','B) Always use API for state setup (creating users, orders, etc.) and reserve UI only for the behavior under test','C) Only when the UI is down','D) Only for performance tests'],answer:1,explanation:'API-driven state setup is orders of magnitude faster than UI navigation. The hybrid principle says: use the API to build preconditions quickly, then test only the UI behavior you actually care about, keeping tests focused and execution times short.'},
{id:4327,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How can Selenium tests verify that a Single Page Application correctly updates the browser history (pushState) without a full reload?',options:['A) By calling driver.navigate().refresh() and checking for data loss','B) By asserting driver.getCurrentUrl() reflects the expected route after user interaction, without page load events firing','C) History API cannot be verified with Selenium','D) By checking the HTTP response code'],answer:1,explanation:'After a `pushState` navigation, `driver.getCurrentUrl()` returns the new URL. Because no HTTP request is made, there is no page load event — tests must wait for DOM changes (new components) with `WebDriverWait` rather than implicit page load completion.'},
{id:4328,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What role does a "Page Component" (sub-page object) play in a large Selenium test suite?',options:['A) It replaces the entire Page Object','B) It encapsulates a reusable UI section (header, nav, modal) shared across multiple Page Objects, reducing duplication','C) It handles database connections','D) It manages WebDriver lifecycle'],answer:1,explanation:'Page Components model repeated UI widgets (navigation bar, date picker, modal dialog) as separate classes. Multiple Page Objects compose them, meaning a change to the shared header widget requires updating only the Header component class.'},
{id:4329,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the purpose of `Network.setCacheDisabled` CDP command in Selenium 4 tests?',options:['A) To clear localStorage','B) To disable browser caching so each test run fetches fresh resources, eliminating cache-related flakiness','C) To throttle network speed','D) To block specific domains'],answer:1,explanation:'`devTools.send(Network.setCacheDisabled(true))` prevents Chrome from serving responses from cache. This is valuable in performance testing (ensuring TTFB is measured for real requests) and in functional tests where stale cached responses could mask regressions.'},
{id:4330,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'When testing a browser extension\'s popup UI with Selenium, how do you open the popup page?',options:['A) Click the extension icon via Actions (coordinates)','B) Navigate directly to the extension\'s chrome-extension://[id]/popup.html URL','C) Extensions popups cannot be tested with Selenium','D) Use driver.switchTo().extension()'],answer:1,explanation:'Chrome extension pages are served from `chrome-extension://[extensionId]/popup.html`. Navigating to this URL directly with `driver.get()` opens the popup as a regular page, allowing full Selenium interaction without needing to click the toolbar icon.'},
{id:4331,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Allure `@Step` annotation used for in Selenium tests?',options:['A) To skip a test step','B) To mark a method as a named step in the Allure report, with optional screenshots and parameters','C) To inject WebDriver','D) To set test priority'],answer:1,explanation:'Methods annotated with `@Step("Description {0}")` appear as named nodes in the Allure report tree. Combined with `@Attachment` screenshots, this creates step-by-step visual documentation of every test action, enormously helpful for failure diagnosis.'},
{id:4332,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does `driver.manage().addCookie()` do?',options:['A) Deletes all cookies','B) Adds a single cookie to the current domain\'s cookie store','C) Reads all cookies from the browser','D) Validates cookie format'],answer:1,explanation:'`addCookie(new Cookie(name, value))` injects a cookie into the browser for the currently loaded domain. It is commonly used to set session tokens, feature flags, or locale preferences without going through the UI.'},
{id:4333,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What technique prevents cross-origin frame access violations when Selenium tries to interact with a third-party embedded iframe?',options:['A) Use the --disable-web-security Chrome flag (test environments only)','B) Set up a reverse proxy to make all origins appear same-origin in test environments','C) Both A and B are valid strategies for isolated test environments','D) Switch to CDP direct commands'],answer:2,explanation:'The same-origin policy blocks cross-origin frame access. In test environments only, `--disable-web-security` removes this restriction. A more robust approach is routing the embedded content through a reverse proxy so all resources share the same origin, mimicking production behavior safely.'},
{id:4334,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In a Gauge specification, how is a parameterized step defined?',options:['A) With XML attributes','B) With inline angle-bracket parameters: `* Login as <username> with password <password>`','C) With JSON placeholders','D) With @Param annotations in the spec file'],answer:1,explanation:'Gauge uses `<paramName>` inline in step text. Values come from a data table below the spec or a CSV file. The corresponding Java implementation receives the values as method arguments, enabling clean data-driven specs.'},
{id:4335,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the Selenium 4 `RelativeLocator.with(By).above(element)` method do?',options:['A) Scrolls up to the element','B) Finds an element positioned above the given reference element in the rendered layout','C) Returns the parent element in the DOM','D) Selects elements with higher z-index'],answer:1,explanation:'Relative locators use the browser\'s rendered positions to find elements spatially. `with(By.tagName("input")).above(submitButton)` returns inputs rendered above the submit button, useful when traditional selectors are fragile.'},
{id:4336,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How does Serenity BDD handle test retry for flaky Selenium tests?',options:['A) It has no retry support','B) Via the `serenity.test.retry.count` property or a custom `@Retryable` extension that re-runs failed scenarios','C) Using TestNG @RetryAnalyzer only','D) By skipping flaky tests automatically'],answer:1,explanation:'Serenity supports retry via `serenity.test.retry.count` in serenity.properties, or by integrating TestNG\'s `@RetryAnalyzer` or JUnit 5 `@RepeatedTest` with Serenity\'s runner. Retried test results are tagged in the report to distinguish genuine failures from flakes.'},
{id:4337,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a "living documentation" report in the context of Serenity BDD?',options:['A) A report that auto-updates every second','B) An HTML report generated from BDD feature files and Selenium execution results that documents both requirements and test outcomes','C) A wiki page manually maintained by QA','D) A Confluence plugin'],answer:1,explanation:'Serenity generates HTML reports that link each BDD scenario (Cucumber/JBehave) to its test result, screenshots, and step details. Because the report is regenerated from executed specifications it always reflects the current state of the software\'s behavior.'},
{id:4338,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the primary benefit of using `SoftAssertions` from AssertJ in a Selenium test?',options:['A) Soft assertions make tests run faster','B) All assertions are collected and reported at the end of the test rather than stopping at the first failure','C) Soft assertions skip failed checks automatically','D) They replace WebDriverWait'],answer:1,explanation:'`SoftAssertions.assertAll()` reports every failed assertion in one go rather than throwing immediately on the first failure. This is valuable for form validation tests where you want to see all field errors at once rather than debugging one at a time.'},
{id:4339,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'When integrating Selenium with a JWT-protected API for hybrid testing, what is the correct flow?',options:['A) Log in via UI, extract the JWT from localStorage via JS, reuse it in RestAssured API calls for state setup','B) Always log in via API first, never via UI','C) Store the JWT in a global static variable permanently','D) JWT cannot be extracted from a browser via Selenium'],answer:0,explanation:'After UI login, `(String) js.executeScript("return localStorage.getItem(\'auth_token\')")` retrieves the JWT. Passing it as a Bearer token header in RestAssured API calls lets the test set up or verify back-end state within the same authenticated session — the essence of hybrid testing.'},
{id:4340,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does "test categorization" in Selenium mean?',options:['A) Naming test files alphabetically','B) Grouping tests into categories (smoke, regression, E2E) so specific subsets can be executed on demand','C) Sorting tests by line count','D) Assigning tests to different browsers'],answer:1,explanation:'Categories/groups (TestNG `groups`, JUnit 5 `@Tag`) allow CI pipelines to run only `smoke` tests on every commit and full `regression` suites nightly, optimizing feedback speed while maintaining thorough coverage.'},
{id:4341,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you configure TestNG to run only tests tagged with the group "smoke"?',options:['A) In the testng.xml file, set <groups><run><include name="smoke"/></run></groups>','B) By setting a system property -Dgroup=smoke in Maven','C) By adding @Smoke annotation from TestNG','D) Smoke groups are not supported in TestNG'],answer:0,explanation:'TestNG\'s XML suite file supports `<groups>` configuration. Including only the "smoke" group means TestNG executes only `@Test(groups={"smoke"})` methods, regardless of what other tests exist in the classes on the classpath.'},
{id:4342,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the difference between E2E tests and integration tests in a Selenium context?',options:['A) No difference','B) E2E tests exercise the entire application stack from UI to database through a real browser; integration tests check component interactions without a full browser','C) E2E tests use mocks; integration tests do not','D) Integration tests always use Selenium; E2E tests do not'],answer:1,explanation:'E2E (end-to-end) tests drive a real browser through real services, validating the complete user journey. Integration tests may stub external dependencies and test subsystem contracts. E2E tests are slower, more realistic, and more brittle than integration tests.'},
{id:4343,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does `PerformanceLoggingPreferences.setTracingCategories()` do in ChromeDriver?',options:['A) Sets the log verbosity level','B) Enables specific Chrome tracing categories (e.g., devtools.timeline) to be captured in performance logs','C) Configures screenshot quality','D) Sets network throttling profiles'],answer:1,explanation:'Chrome\'s tracing system captures detailed timing data per category. Setting tracing categories via `PerformanceLoggingPreferences` and enabling it in `ChromeOptions` provides raw JSON trace data useful for performance analysis of rendering, scripting, and painting.'},
{id:4344,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you configure ChromeOptions to disable the "Save password" popup in automated tests?',options:['A) prefs.put("credentials_enable_service", false) and prefs.put("profile.password_manager_enabled", false)','B) ChromeOptions.addArguments("--no-password-save")','C) Set a WebDriver capability "password.disabled" to true','D) Password popups cannot be disabled'],answer:0,explanation:'Chrome user preferences `credentials_enable_service: false` and `profile.password_manager_enabled: false` suppress the password save bubble. These are passed via `ChromeOptions.setExperimentalOption("prefs", prefsMap)`, preventing dialog interference in tests.'},
{id:4345,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the purpose of the `--remote-allow-origins=*` flag when starting ChromeDriver 111+?',options:['A) Allows Selenium Grid to connect to any hub','B) Removes the CORS origin check on the ChromeDriver HTTP server, required when WebDriver client and ChromeDriver are on different hosts','C) Enables cross-origin iframe access','D) Disables SSL certificate validation'],answer:1,explanation:'ChromeDriver 111 introduced origin enforcement on its local HTTP server. When running tests on a remote machine or in Docker, the request origin differs from `localhost`, causing 403 errors. `--remote-allow-origins=*` disables this check for test infrastructure.'},
{id:4346,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In an internationalization test, how do you verify that a numeric value is formatted according to locale (e.g., 1,000.00 vs 1.000,00)?',options:['A) Compare the raw API response number directly','B) Get the text of the formatted element and assert against the locale-specific pattern (regex or exact string)','C) Change the JVM locale and rerun','D) Use a math assertion on the double value'],answer:1,explanation:'Locale-specific number formatting must be verified at the UI layer. A regex like `\\d{1,3}(\\.\\d{3})*(,\\d{2})?` for German locale asserts the display format. Comparing to the API raw number misses rendering bugs entirely.'},
{id:4347,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the purpose of `driver.navigate().back()` in a Selenium test?',options:['A) Closes the browser','B) Navigates to the previous page in the browser history, equivalent to clicking the Back button','C) Reloads the current page','D) Clears the browser cache'],answer:1,explanation:'`driver.navigate().back()` triggers a browser history navigation backward. It is useful in E2E tests that need to verify that pressing back retains or resets form state, and that the previous page is rendered correctly after forward navigation.'},
{id:4348,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the `Network.requestIntercepted` CDP event enable in Selenium 4 tests?',options:['A) Blocking specific network requests or modifying request/response headers in flight','B) Logging all requests to a file','C) Throttling individual requests','D) It only works with Firefox'],answer:0,explanation:'`Network.requestIntercepted` fires before requests are sent, allowing the test to block, modify headers, return mock responses, or continue normally. This is used to test app behavior under API failures, inject auth headers, or mock third-party services.'},
{id:4349,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'When testing a drag-and-drop reorderable list (e.g., sortable.js) with Selenium, which approach is most reliable?',options:['A) Actions.dragAndDropBy with precise pixel offsets obtained from element.getLocation() and element.getSize()','B) Double-clicking the element','C) Using keyboard arrow keys','D) Injecting JS to directly manipulate the sortable data model'],answer:0,explanation:'For HTML5 sortables, calculating the center coordinates of both source and target elements and using `dragAndDropBy` with offset values from `getLocation()`/`getSize()` is the most reliable WebDriver approach. JS model injection is an alternative but bypasses UI rendering validation.'},
{id:4350,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What JUnit 5 annotation enables running the same Selenium test with multiple parameter sets?',options:['A) @RepeatedTest','B) @ParameterizedTest with @MethodSource or @CsvSource','C) @DataDriven','D) @MultiRun'],answer:1,explanation:'`@ParameterizedTest` combined with `@CsvSource`, `@MethodSource`, or `@ValueSource` runs the test method once per argument set. For Selenium, `@MethodSource` pointing to a method that returns a stream of browser/data combinations is the typical pattern.'},
{id:4351,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the key advantage of using `RestAssured` alongside Selenium in hybrid tests?',options:['A) RestAssured can replace Selenium for UI testing','B) RestAssured provides a fluent API for HTTP requests, enabling fast back-end state validation and setup without browser navigation','C) RestAssured is a Selenium plugin','D) RestAssured generates locators automatically'],answer:1,explanation:'RestAssured\'s fluent DSL (`given().header(...).when().post(...).then().statusCode(200)`) enables type-safe API calls. In hybrid tests it creates preconditions (POST /users) or validates side effects (GET /orders) faster and more reliably than navigating UI screens.'},
{id:4352,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How can a Selenium test verify that a keyboard-navigable modal traps focus correctly (focus does not escape to the background)?',options:['A) By checking z-index','B) By sending TAB repeatedly and verifying that activeElement() always remains within the modal\'s DOM subtree','C) By asserting aria-modal="true" only','D) Focus trapping cannot be tested with Selenium'],answer:1,explanation:'A correctly implemented focus trap keeps Tab/Shift+Tab cycling within the modal. Sending `Keys.TAB` N+1 times and asserting `driver.switchTo().activeElement()` is always a descendant of the modal container validates the ARIA/focus trap implementation programmatically.'},
{id:4353,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the role of a `TestWatcher` (JUnit 5) in a Selenium test suite?',options:['A) To retry failed tests','B) To hook into test lifecycle events (testFailed, testSucceeded) to perform actions like taking screenshots on failure','C) To parameterize tests','D) To manage WebDriver sessions'],answer:1,explanation:'`TestWatcher` implements `testFailed(ExtensionContext, Throwable)` which fires when a test fails. A common implementation takes a screenshot and saves it with the test name, providing visual evidence without requiring try/catch in every test method.'},
{id:4354,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the difference between "logical" and "visual" locator strategies in AI-assisted tools like Applitools UFG?',options:['A) Logical locators use XPath; visual uses screenshots','B) Logical locators identify elements by DOM attributes; visual locators use pixel/layout matching to find elements by appearance across browsers','C) Visual locators only work on mobile','D) There is no difference in Applitools'],answer:1,explanation:'Applitools Ultrafast Grid uses visual matching — comparing rendered screenshots using AI — rather than DOM attribute matching. This detects visual regressions invisible to text/attribute assertions, such as overlapping elements, color contrast issues, or font rendering differences.'},
{id:4355,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In a Selenium performance log, what type of entry indicates a slow resource load?',options:['A) Network.requestWillBeSent with a very short duration','B) Network.loadingFinished entries where encodedDataLength is large and duration (responseReceived - requestWillBeSent) is high','C) Console.error entries','D) Page.frameNavigated entries'],answer:1,explanation:'Performance log entries include timing for each resource. Parsing `Network.loadingFinished` timestamps relative to `Network.requestWillBeSent` yields individual resource load times. High values indicate slow assets impacting page performance during test execution.'},
{id:4356,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does the `By.linkText("Click here")` locator do?',options:['A) Finds any element containing the text "Click here"','B) Finds an anchor element whose complete visible text is exactly "Click here"','C) Finds elements by their href attribute value','D) Finds elements by their CSS class'],answer:1,explanation:'`By.linkText` matches `<a>` tags whose full visible text equals the provided string exactly (case-sensitive). Use `By.partialLinkText` for substring matching. It is simple but fragile when link text is dynamic or localized.'},
{id:4357,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does Selenium 4 DevTools support differ between RemoteWebDriver (Grid) and local ChromeDriver?',options:['A) DevTools is only available locally','B) Selenium 4 introduced `HasDevTools` interface supported by both local ChromeDriver and RemoteWebDriver when connected to Grid 4 nodes, using WebSocket tunneling','C) Grid 4 blocks all CDP commands','D) RemoteWebDriver uses a different DevTools API version'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium Grid 4 tunnels CDP commands via WebSocket through the Grid Hub/Router to the actual node, so `((HasDevTools) driver).getDevTools()` works transparently on remote sessions. Grid 3 and Selenium 3 did not support this.'},
{id:4358,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the purpose of `Page.addScriptToEvaluateOnNewDocument` CDP command in test automation?',options:['A) To add a script tag to the DOM','B) To inject JavaScript that runs before any page script on every navigation, useful for mocking globals or overriding APIs in tests','C) To disable JavaScript on the page','D) To capture page source'],answer:1,explanation:'This CDP command injects JS into the page context before any other scripts execute. Tests use it to replace `Date.now()` for deterministic time, mock `window.fetch`, or set global test flags — without modifying application source code.'},
{id:4359,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the common approach to test PDF generation without opening a PDF viewer in Selenium?',options:['A) Use OCR to read the PDF viewer','B) Configure Chrome to print to PDF via CDP\'s Page.printToPDF and validate the resulting PDF bytes programmatically','C) PDFs cannot be validated in Selenium tests','D) Always use a dedicated PDF testing tool outside Selenium'],answer:1,explanation:'`Page.printToPDF` returns base64-encoded PDF bytes via CDP. Decoding them and using Apache PDFBox extracts text, enabling assertions on PDF content without browser UI interaction or filesystem file-download polling.'},
{id:4360,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Selenium `EventFiringWebDriver` (Selenium 3) / `EventFiringDecorator` (Selenium 4) used for?',options:['A) Firing mouse events manually','B) Wrapping WebDriver to intercept and log method calls (beforeClickOn, afterFindBy, etc.) for debugging, reporting, or screenshot capture','C) Running tests in parallel','D) Connecting to Selenium Grid'],answer:1,explanation:'`EventFiringDecorator` wraps any WebDriver instance and notifies registered `WebDriverListener` implementations before/after each WebDriver action. This enables cross-cutting concerns like logging, screenshots on click, or timing measurements without modifying test/page code.'},
{id:4361,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you verify clipboard content in a headless Chrome Selenium test where navigator.clipboard requires permissions?',options:['A) Grant clipboard-read/write permissions via CDP Browser.grantPermissions before reading clipboard','B) Clipboard is inaccessible in headless mode','C) Use keyboard shortcuts only','D) Read clipboard from the OS via Robot class'],answer:0,explanation:'`Browser.grantPermissions(["clipboard-read","clipboard-write"], origin)` via CDP grants the clipboard permission to the test origin in headless mode. After granting, `navigator.clipboard.readText()` works as expected when executed via JavascriptExecutor.'},
{id:4362,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the `Augmenter` class do in Selenium 4?',options:['A) Increases screenshot resolution','B) Enhances a RemoteWebDriver instance with additional interfaces (HasDevTools, HasNetwork, TakesScreenshot) at runtime','C) Augments test reports with extra data','D) Converts WebDriver to BiDi protocol'],answer:1,explanation:'`new Augmenter().augment(driver)` inspects the remote session\'s capabilities and returns a driver cast-able to extended interfaces. Without augmenting, a `RemoteWebDriver` cannot be cast to `HasDevTools` even if the remote node supports CDP.'},
{id:4363,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a "test data cleanup" anti-pattern that Selenium teams commonly fall into?',options:['A) Cleaning up after every test','B) Relying on the test setup (@BeforeEach) to clean old data instead of cleaning in @AfterEach, leaving dirty data if setup itself fails','C) Using API calls for cleanup','D) Deleting only data created by the current test'],answer:1,explanation:'Putting cleanup only in `@BeforeEach` means if a previous test crashed before its setup ran, stale data persists indefinitely. Best practice is idempotent cleanup in `@AfterEach` (or both), ensuring each test leaves the environment clean regardless of execution order.'},
{id:4364,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What Chrome DevTools Protocol domain is used to intercept and mock network responses in Selenium 4 tests?',options:['A) Fetch (preferred) or Network domain','B) Page domain','C) Runtime domain','D) Target domain'],answer:0,explanation:'`Fetch.enable` with `requestStage: Response` allows intercepting requests before they complete. `Fetch.fulfillRequest` returns a mocked response body and status. The `Network` domain\'s `setRequestInterception` is the older alternative; `Fetch` domain is the current recommended approach.'},
{id:4365,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is a "Page Object" in Selenium?',options:['A) A WebElement representing a full page','B) A class that models a page or component, encapsulating locators and interaction methods, separating test logic from UI details','C) A Selenium Grid configuration file','D) A browser window handle'],answer:1,explanation:'Page Object Model (POM) separates WebElement locators and interaction methods from test logic. Each page or major component has its own class. Tests call meaningful methods like `loginPage.login(user, pass)` rather than raw `findElement(By.id("...")).sendKeys(...)`.'},
{id:4366,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does `WebDriverListener` in Selenium 4 differ from `WebDriverEventListener` in Selenium 3?',options:['A) They are identical','B) `WebDriverListener` uses default interface methods, so implementations only override the specific callbacks they need, reducing boilerplate','C) `WebDriverListener` does not support beforeClick','D) `WebDriverEventListener` supports more events'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium 3\'s `AbstractWebDriverEventListener` required implementing all methods (even empty ones). Selenium 4\'s `WebDriverListener` interface uses Java 8 default methods, so a listener class only declares the hooks it cares about, resulting in cleaner, more maintainable code.'},
{id:4367,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How would you automate a SAML SSO login flow in Selenium without hitting the actual Identity Provider?',options:['A) Mock the SAML IdP by running a local SAML stub (e.g., a WireMock + custom SAML response generator) that the app trusts','B) SAML cannot be automated with Selenium','C) Use the Selenium Grid for SAML','D) Disable SAML in the app for testing'],answer:0,explanation:'A local SAML stub (e.g., custom Spring Security SAML config pointing to a test IdP metadata, or stub-idp library) generates signed SAML assertions without real user credentials. The app is configured to trust the test IdP, allowing full E2E automation of SAML-protected flows.'},
{id:4368,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of `driver.manage().window().setSize(new Dimension(390,844))` in Selenium tests?',options:['A) Maximizes the browser window','B) Sets the browser window to iPhone 14 Pro resolution for responsive/mobile layout testing','C) Sets DPI scaling','D) Changes the browser zoom level'],answer:1,explanation:'Setting explicit window dimensions allows testing responsive breakpoints. At 390×844 pixels, the app should render its mobile layout. Combined with a mobile user agent string, this simulates a mobile browser environment for desktop Selenium tests.'},
{id:4369,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the challenge of testing RTL (right-to-left) text truncation in a Selenium test?',options:['A) WebDriver cannot read RTL text','B) CSS text-overflow:ellipsis may truncate from the left in RTL mode; visual validation or checking scrollWidth vs offsetWidth is needed','C) RTL truncation is identical to LTR','D) Truncation only occurs in WebKit browsers'],answer:1,explanation:'In RTL text truncation, the ellipsis appears on the left side. Verifying this requires either visual comparison (screenshot) or checking `scrollWidth > offsetWidth` via JS to detect overflow, combined with CSS `direction:rtl` and `text-overflow:ellipsis` assertions.'},
{id:4370,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What Selenium approach is used to verify that a date picker correctly handles locale-specific date formats?',options:['A) Assert the raw input value attribute','B) Interact with the date picker as a user would, then assert the displayed and submitted date values match the locale format (e.g., DD/MM/YYYY vs MM/DD/YYYY)','C) Date pickers cannot be tested with Selenium','D) Set the system locale before the test'],answer:1,explanation:'Locale-aware date pickers render and accept dates differently per locale. Tests should enter a date, submit, and assert both the displayed format and the submitted API payload (intercepted via CDP or verified server-side) match the expected locale format.'},
{id:4371,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In Selenium Grid 4 with Kubernetes, what is the role of the Selenium "Session Map" component?',options:['A) It stores test results','B) It maps session IDs to the specific Node URL handling that session, enabling the Router to proxy requests to the correct node','C) It manages browser binaries','D) It handles authentication to the Grid'],answer:1,explanation:'The Session Map is a distributed key-value store (Redis-backed in clustered deployments) that records which Grid Node owns each active session. The Router queries it to route subsequent WebDriver commands to the correct node.'},
{id:4372,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of the `driver.manage().logs().get(LogType.BROWSER)` call in Selenium?',options:['A) Gets server-side application logs','B) Retrieves browser console log entries (errors, warnings, info) captured since the last call','C) Returns screenshot logs','D) Fetches Selenium Grid logs'],answer:1,explanation:'Browser logs capture `console.error()`, `console.warn()`, and uncaught JS exceptions. Asserting that `driver.manage().logs().get(LogType.BROWSER)` contains no SEVERE entries is a zero-cost way to detect JS errors introduced by new deployments.'},
{id:4373,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What consideration is critical when running Selenium tests across time zones for internationalization?',options:['A) Browser time zone has no effect on test results','B) Browser/OS time zone affects date rendering, relative timestamps ("2 hours ago"), and deadline calculations — tests should set the TZ explicitly via CDP Emulation.setTimezoneOverride','C) Only the server time zone matters','D) Time zone issues only affect mobile testing'],answer:1,explanation:'`Emulation.setTimezoneOverride(timezoneId)` via CDP sets the browser\'s perceived time zone. This is essential for testing date-sensitive UI (e.g., "Today", calendar views) across time zones without changing the CI server\'s system clock.'},
{id:4374,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the difference between `findElement` and `findElements` in Selenium?',options:['A) findElements is faster','B) findElement returns a single WebElement (throws NoSuchElementException if not found); findElements returns a List (empty list if none found)','C) findElements only works with CSS selectors','D) They are identical'],answer:1,explanation:'`findElement` throws `NoSuchElementException` immediately if no match is found, while `findElements` returns an empty `List<WebElement>`. Use `findElements` when checking for element presence (list size > 0) to avoid exception-based control flow.'},
{id:4375,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does `driver.executeAsyncScript()` do differently from `driver.executeScript()`?',options:['A) executeAsyncScript runs faster','B) executeAsyncScript injects a callback argument and waits for it to be called before returning control to WebDriver, enabling async JS operations','C) executeAsyncScript blocks the browser thread','D) They are identical'],answer:1,explanation:'`executeAsyncScript` provides a `callback` as the last argument. The script must call `callback(result)` when done. WebDriver waits until the callback is called or the async script timeout expires, enabling coordination with asynchronous browser operations like animations or fetch calls.'},
{id:4376,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How can Selenium tests assert that an application correctly implements Content Security Policy (CSP) by detecting CSP violation reports?',options:['A) By checking the HTML meta tag','B) By subscribing to CDP Network events and looking for CSP violation report requests (to the report-uri endpoint) or Security.securityStateChanged events','C) CSP violations are not detectable in Selenium','D) By checking response headers directly'],answer:1,explanation:'CSP violations trigger a `SecurityViolationEvent` in the browser and optionally POST to the `report-uri`. Selenium can intercept these outgoing POSTs via CDP `Fetch.enable` or monitor `Security.securityStateChanged` events to assert no inline script violations occur during test execution.'},
{id:4377,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In a CI/CD pipeline, what does a "test execution time trend" dashboard reveal?',options:['A) Which developers wrote the most tests','B) Whether test suite execution time is growing over time, indicating the need for parallelization or test pruning','C) The number of unique users tested','D) Network bandwidth usage'],answer:1,explanation:'Tracking suite execution time across builds (Grafana + Prometheus or Allure history) shows whether growth in test count is slowing CI. Teams use this to trigger parallelization strategies or identify slow tests that disproportionately impact total build time.'},
{id:4378,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the benefit of using `Selenoid` over standard Selenium Grid for video recording?',options:['A) Selenoid cannot record video','B) Selenoid natively supports per-session video recording via its capabilities (enableVideo: true), producing MP4 files accessible through the Selenoid UI without additional tools','C) Selenoid requires Monte Screen Recorder','D) Selenoid video recording requires GPU acceleration'],answer:1,explanation:'Adding `"enableVideo": true` to Selenoid capabilities activates built-in FFmpeg-based recording for the session. Videos are stored on the Selenoid server and browsable via Selenoid UI, with no test-side recorder setup — far simpler than integrating Monte Screen Recorder.'},
{id:4379,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which Selenium 4 capability is used to set a browser locale at the browser level (not just the app)?',options:['A) ChromeOptions.addArguments("--lang=de-DE")','B) driver.manage().setLocale("de-DE")','C) DesiredCapabilities.locale("de-DE")','D) ChromeOptions.addArguments("--accept-language=de-DE")'],answer:3,explanation:'`--accept-language=de-DE` sets the HTTP `Accept-Language` header Chrome sends with every request, signaling the desired language to the server. Combined with `--lang=de-DE` (which sets the browser UI language), this fully simulates a German-language browser session.'},
{id:4380,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'When testing localization, what is "pseudo-localization" and why is it useful in Selenium tests?',options:['A) A fake locale used to test currency formatting','B) A transformation of English strings (e.g., "Héllo Wörld!") that stresses UI layout for i18n issues (text expansion, character encoding) without full translation','C) Using machine translation in tests','D) Testing with only ASCII characters'],answer:1,explanation:'Pseudo-localization replaces ASCII characters with accented equivalents and adds padding (~30% length increase) to simulate translated text length. Selenium tests against a pseudo-localized build will catch UI overflow, truncation, and encoding bugs before real translations are available.'},
{id:4381,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the function of the `Selenium Manager` tool introduced in Selenium 4.6+?',options:['A) It manages Selenium Grid nodes','B) It automatically downloads and caches the correct browser driver binary (ChromeDriver, GeckoDriver, etc.) matching the installed browser version','C) It manages test execution order','D) It generates Page Objects automatically'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium Manager eliminates the need for WebDriverManager or manual driver downloads. It detects the installed browser version and downloads the matching driver binary automatically, simplifying local and CI setup without extra dependencies.'},
{id:4382,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you assert that an HTTP-only cookie cannot be read via JavaScript, confirming the HttpOnly flag is set?',options:['A) driver.manage().getCookieNamed("session").isHttpOnly()','B) Execute document.cookie via JS and assert the cookie name does NOT appear in the returned string','C) Both A and B together provide full confirmation','D) HttpOnly cannot be tested with Selenium'],answer:2,explanation:'`getCookieNamed("session").isHttpOnly()` reads the flag from the WebDriver cookie API (which has server-side knowledge), while `(String) js.executeScript("return document.cookie")` confirms the cookie is absent from JS-accessible cookies — together they fully validate HttpOnly behavior.'},
{id:4383,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the purpose of `Emulation.setDeviceMetricsOverride` in Selenium 4 CDP integration?',options:['A) To change the CPU speed','B) To override screen width, height, deviceScaleFactor, and mobile flag — simulating a specific device viewport in Chrome','C) To set network speed','D) To change the GPU renderer'],answer:1,explanation:'`Emulation.setDeviceMetricsOverride` combined with `Emulation.setUserAgentOverride` fully emulates a specific device in Chrome. Width, height, scale factor, and mobile boolean are set per device spec (e.g., iPhone 14: 390×844, scale 3, mobile true).'},
{id:4384,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a "flaky test" pass rate threshold commonly used to trigger a test quarantine policy?',options:['A) Any test that fails once is quarantined','B) Tests failing more than 10-20% of runs (without code changes) are commonly flagged for quarantine or investigation','C) 50% failure rate','D) Flaky tests are never quarantined'],answer:1,explanation:'A common policy quarantines tests with a flakiness rate above 10–20% over a rolling window. Quarantined tests are excluded from the blocking gate while being tracked on a separate flaky-test dashboard, preventing false CI failures while ensuring the instability is investigated.'},
{id:4385,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How can Selenium 4 interact with a WebExtension\'s background service worker for testing?',options:['A) Via driver.switchTo().serviceWorker()','B) By using CDP Target.getTargets to find the extension background target, then attaching a separate session to it via Target.attachToTarget','C) Background service workers cannot be accessed by WebDriver','D) Via driver.executeScript("serviceWorker.postMessage()")'],answer:1,explanation:'Chrome exposes extension background pages/service workers as separate CDP targets. `Target.getTargets()` lists them by `type: "background_page"` or `"service_worker"`. Attaching with `Target.attachToTarget` gives a separate DevTools session for evaluating scripts and inspecting state.'},
{id:4386,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In the context of Selenium maintenance KPIs, what does "mean time to repair" (MTTR) for failing tests measure?',options:['A) The average time a test takes to run','B) The average time from a test failure being detected to the test being fixed and green again','C) The frequency of test failures','D) The time to set up the test environment'],answer:1,explanation:'MTTR for tests measures responsiveness of the QA/dev team to test failures. A high MTTR means failures stay in the suite unaddressed, eroding team confidence in the test suite signal. Tracking MTTR per team or component motivates prompt failure triage.'},
{id:4387,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the recommended pattern for managing WebDriver instance lifecycle in a parallel JUnit 5 + Selenium suite without thread-safety issues?',options:['A) Use a single static WebDriver instance','B) Store WebDriver in a `ThreadLocal<WebDriver>` initialized in @BeforeEach and removed/quit in @AfterEach per thread','C) Create a new WebDriver for every test class only','D) Share WebDriver via a synchronized singleton'],answer:1,explanation:'`ThreadLocal<WebDriver>` ensures each parallel test thread has its own isolated driver instance. Initializing in `@BeforeEach` and calling `driver.quit(); threadLocal.remove()` in `@AfterEach` prevents session leaks and cross-thread interference, which is the standard pattern for parallel Selenium.'},
{id:4388,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you measure navigation timing (DOMContentLoaded, load event) using Selenium and the browser Navigation Timing API?',options:['A) driver.manage().timeouts().pageLoadTimeout()','B) Execute `JSON.stringify(window.performance.timing)` via JavascriptExecutor and parse the resulting JSON for timing values','C) Use a Selenium built-in performance class','D) Navigation timing is only available via JMeter'],answer:1,explanation:'`window.performance.timing` exposes timestamps for all navigation milestones. `(String) js.executeScript("return JSON.stringify(window.performance.timing)")` retrieves them. `domContentLoadedEventEnd - navigationStart` gives DOMContentLoaded time; `loadEventEnd - navigationStart` gives full load time.'},
{id:4389,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the correct way to use Selenium with the Gauge framework for a scenario that requires multiple browser sessions?',options:['A) Gauge does not support multiple sessions','B) Create multiple WebDriver instances as separate context variables in Gauge\'s execution context, initializing and managing each in BeforeScenario/AfterScenario hooks','C) Use Gauge\'s built-in multi-session API','D) Open multiple windows in a single WebDriver instance'],answer:1,explanation:'Gauge\'s `ExecutionContext` and `@BeforeScenario` hooks allow creating and storing multiple `WebDriver` instances (e.g., buyer and seller sessions). Step implementations reference the correct instance by role, enabling multi-actor test scenarios with fully isolated browser states.'},
{id:4390,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'When comparing Selenide, Serenity BDD, and FluentSelenium for a large enterprise project, what is the deciding factor for choosing Serenity BDD?',options:['A) Serenity generates the most concise code','B) Serenity is best when the project requires living BDD documentation, stakeholder-readable reports, and traceability between requirements and test results','C) Serenity has the best selector API','D) Serenity requires no configuration'],answer:1,explanation:'Serenity excels in regulated, documentation-heavy environments where business stakeholders need readable test reports linked to requirements. Selenide wins on developer ergonomics and conciseness; FluentSelenium on minimal dependency overhead. The living documentation requirement uniquely justifies Serenity\'s additional complexity.'}
]);
